WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:01.600
- [Gary] Guys, say
hello to my friend Jess.

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.734
(audience cheers and applause)

00:00:07.867 --> 00:00:11.500
So, thanks guys for hanging.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.133
So I emailed Jess the other day.

00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:16.467
I was like, "Hey, if you
come through to New York,

00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:18.800
"I'd love to do a
fireside chat with the company."

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.033
I want to do more of
these with you guys and

00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:22.567
I couldn't of
thought of a better guest.

00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:25.567
Obviously, we got to know
each other quite a bit filming

00:00:25.567 --> 00:00:28.533
Planet of the Apps but I've
always known from afar when we

00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:30.967
ran into each other a couple
non-profit events through the

00:00:30.967 --> 00:00:36.100
years of how impressive of a
operator and an entrepreneur and

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.867
a thinker about marketing,
media and business

00:00:38.867 --> 00:00:42.300
Jess has been for
quite a long time.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.633
Obviously, she had real fame
before that world and so

00:00:46.633 --> 00:00:48.333
I don't think a lot of
people, you know,

00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:50.233
that are paying
attention realize it.

00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:53.467
And then I got to really see it
on display from a business chops

00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:54.433
standpoint on the show.

00:00:54.433 --> 00:00:57.367
Obviously, the show is very
real and like we were critiquing

00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:00.200
businesses and asking questions.
- Yeah. 

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.100
- I really enjoyed it and
I'm just extremely fond of her

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:07.633
so one more time please clap it
up for somebody I respect.

00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:10.266
(audience
applauds enthusiastically)

00:01:12.233 --> 00:01:14.166
So I think I'm gonna
ask a couple questions

00:01:14.166 --> 00:01:15.767
and then I think
we'll open it up to

00:01:15.767 --> 00:01:18.600
some questions and then
we'll all go on our merry ways.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:23.767
So, first and foremost Jess,
when did you start thinking

00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:28.066
about or realize that
you had business interests?

00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:32.967
- Hmmm.

00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:36.567
I think I always
approached my career,

00:01:36.567 --> 00:01:39.800
even when I was 12
when I started acting,

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.734
I was like what's the long game?

00:01:42.734 --> 00:01:47.333
How do I stay relevant and how
do I become a real player and

00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:50.133
how am I going to be meaningful?

00:01:50.133 --> 00:01:53.900
And so I was always
pretty strategic in the way that

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:57.667
I approached my career

00:01:57.667 --> 00:01:59.767
and I thought of
myself as a brand

00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:03.233
before it was a thing to
think of yourself as a brand.

00:02:03.233 --> 00:02:06.600
I was always really cognizant
that anything I associated

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.834
myself with was going to be

00:02:07.834 --> 00:02:09.834
reflective of how
people thought of me.

00:02:09.834 --> 00:02:10.600
- Yep.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.400
- And I protected that 

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.066
for a long time

00:02:16.066 --> 00:02:18.667
and when I decided to

00:02:18.667 --> 00:02:20.934
launch my own brand 

00:02:20.934 --> 00:02:22.900
I really feel like

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:26.233
all of that thought

00:02:26.233 --> 00:02:28.900
really got channeled in
in the best way.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:32.767
I didn't know what the outcome
would be but I was certainly

00:02:32.767 --> 00:02:37.667
very aware of being protective
of my image from day one.

00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:39.600
- So on that note, I'm
actually remembering this from a

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.100
conversation now,
let's dig a little bit deeper.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:46.200
Was there ever a role that you
really jockeyed for or strategic

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.266
for or was there anything that
CAA and those characters were

00:02:50.266 --> 00:02:55.367
pushing you hard to do that you
passed on based on this thesis?

00:02:55.367 --> 00:02:57.233
- Yeah.
- Tell us.

00:02:57.233 --> 00:02:59.033
- Both.
(audience laughter)

00:02:59.033 --> 00:02:59.834
- Everything.

00:02:59.834 --> 00:03:02.934
- So I never got to really show

00:03:02.934 --> 00:03:06.400
my chops as a
really dramatic actress

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.667
when I was young because
I wouldn't show my boobs.

00:03:08.667 --> 00:03:10.266
(Gary and audience laugh)

00:03:10.266 --> 00:03:12.734
All the good
roles were sexy time

00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:16.533
and I was like,
I for whatever reason

00:03:16.533 --> 00:03:19.033
I felt like when I was

00:03:19.033 --> 00:03:23.567
young it would just be exploited

00:03:23.567 --> 00:03:26.533
in a way and out of my control

00:03:26.533 --> 00:03:29.867
in a way that I felt wouldn't

00:03:29.867 --> 00:03:33.033
be great for my brand before

00:03:33.033 --> 00:03:35.667
I really
articulated it that way.

00:03:35.667 --> 00:03:39.567
And I also grew up
Catholic and Christian,

00:03:39.567 --> 00:03:43.500
I couldn't ever deal with my
dad seeing me do that stuff.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:44.934
- Dad.
- My dad's here.

00:03:44.934 --> 00:03:47.266
- That's true.
(audience laughter)

00:03:47.266 --> 00:03:48.700
- And so I grew up with the

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.100
guilt and the things and the

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.266
weirdness and so
that played into it.

00:03:53.266 --> 00:03:58.467
But yeah,
I definitely took more 

00:03:58.467 --> 00:03:59.867
or passed up on a lot of

00:03:59.867 --> 00:04:02.400
opportunities that would've

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.700
positioned me as an actress in

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.233
Hollywood in a different way.

00:04:08.233 --> 00:04:10.700
Maybe in a more serious way.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.300
But on the flip side,

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:16.300
I went the route of
wanting to be global.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:18.600
Wanting to--

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.233
- So is that
like superhero time?

00:04:20.233 --> 00:04:21.266
Is that that?

00:04:21.266 --> 00:04:23.834
- Yeah, I looked at the
marketplace and I was like

00:04:23.834 --> 00:04:25.166
what are the
things that go global?

00:04:25.166 --> 00:04:27.266
And it was

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:31.834
superhero franchises, action,

00:04:31.834 --> 00:04:35.066
it was pre-horror and 

00:04:35.066 --> 00:04:38.700
it was pre-rom com being global.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:42.734
And so rom com was to make
you America's Sweetheart but it

00:04:42.734 --> 00:04:44.767
really didn't extend beyond that

00:04:44.767 --> 00:04:46.367
but you can get a decent payday

00:04:46.367 --> 00:04:48.467
and you can be the
face of a beauty brand.

00:04:48.467 --> 00:04:49.533
Fine.

00:04:49.533 --> 00:04:54.133
Or you can transcend
that, the girl stuff,

00:04:54.133 --> 00:04:58.834
and you can go into boy land
and you can do action hero

00:04:58.834 --> 00:05:01.400
and I wanted to
be an action hero.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.000
- 'Cause you wanted to collect
yourself as an action figure?

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.867
- Well, yeah.
(audience laughter)

00:05:05.867 --> 00:05:07.867
- Of course.
- I have a few.

00:05:07.867 --> 00:05:09.467
(audience laughter)

00:05:09.467 --> 00:05:13.233
So yeah, I've been in many
action figures over the years.

00:05:13.233 --> 00:05:16.266
When I started with a TV show
I did with James Cameron called

00:05:16.266 --> 00:05:18.066
"Dark Angel" I was
actually offered--

00:05:18.066 --> 00:05:21.233
- Oh, we remember.
(audience laughter)

00:05:21.233 --> 00:05:25.667
- I was 17 and I was like if I
don't get real role by the time

00:05:25.667 --> 00:05:29.467
I'm 18 that's going to really
make me feel comfortable with

00:05:29.467 --> 00:05:33.867
choosing this as a path as a
career than I'm going to go to

00:05:33.867 --> 00:05:38.834
school and get a
degree and have a real job.

00:05:38.834 --> 00:05:41.367
- So really at that point in
your career you were like okay--

00:05:41.367 --> 00:05:45.767
- So between 16 and 18 I gave
myself two years to really kind

00:05:45.767 --> 00:05:49.734
of figure out if this was
supposed to be my path in life

00:05:49.734 --> 00:05:53.533
and I was offered a role in
"The Fast and The Furious" and

00:05:53.533 --> 00:05:56.967
I was also offered
the starring role of

00:05:56.967 --> 00:06:01.066
James Cameron's first TV show

00:06:01.066 --> 00:06:04.166
his first project
after he did Titanic.

00:06:06.533 --> 00:06:10.233
I took the James Cameron one.

00:06:10.233 --> 00:06:12.300
And then from there I
was like,

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:15.767
"Alright, I guess these are the
stars aligning telling me that

00:06:15.767 --> 00:06:20.467
"I should maybe
do this for a living."

00:06:20.467 --> 00:06:24.934
So yeah, from there I just
thought strategically about how

00:06:24.934 --> 00:06:28.934
to maintain that
global awareness

00:06:28.934 --> 00:06:31.266
and I didn't want to

00:06:31.266 --> 00:06:34.200
be the girl to the guy
that put asses in seats,

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:36.867
I wanted to be the
girl who put asses in seats.

00:06:36.867 --> 00:06:39.100
And the way to do that was with

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:41.567
action franchises at the time.

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:43.633
- Very cool. So, how do
you think about it now?

00:06:43.633 --> 00:06:45.333
Are you out?

00:06:45.333 --> 00:06:46.166
You gonna act again?

00:06:46.166 --> 00:06:47.633
Where are you in--

00:06:47.633 --> 00:06:48.467
- Well, it's tough.

00:06:48.467 --> 00:06:52.867
I really haven't focused on
this business for nine years.

00:06:52.867 --> 00:06:54.000
- Has it been that, really?

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:54.934
- It's been a long time.

00:06:54.934 --> 00:06:56.500
- When you say that,
what does that mean?

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:58.066
It's been nine years since what?

00:06:58.066 --> 00:07:00.600
- Since I really
cared about it--

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:01.800
- Like that way. (laughs)

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:02.633
Okay.

00:07:02.633 --> 00:07:04.400
- Well, no, like--
- Since you put, yeah

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:05.700
- my focus, I sort of--

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:07.000
- Well, you've
done stuff, right?

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:11.533
- Yeah, but I got pregnant
and I had a kid at 27,

00:07:11.533 --> 00:07:15.033
I got pregnant at
26 and had her at 27.

00:07:15.033 --> 00:07:18.033
And I'd been working
for so long, at that point,

00:07:18.033 --> 00:07:20.567
I'd been working since I was 12,

00:07:20.567 --> 00:07:24.800
and hardcore
really since I was 17 so,

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:27.600
it was a solid 10 years of
not having a personal life.

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:28.200
- Yep.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:33.066
- And not really having
any life outside of working

00:07:33.066 --> 00:07:36.500
90 hour weeks often.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:38.867
So, I didn't sleep much,
and I worked a lot,

00:07:38.867 --> 00:07:41.600
and it was a blessing because
it was a dream come true.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:45.633
But then, when I actually sat in
one place for the first time,

00:07:45.633 --> 00:07:48.967
and I had a person
growing in my stomach,

00:07:48.967 --> 00:07:51.967
and I was like oh my
goodness, she's gonna rely on me

00:07:51.967 --> 00:07:53.867
to stay alive.

00:07:53.867 --> 00:07:55.533
(audience laughs)

00:07:55.533 --> 00:07:57.900
- That is true.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.500
- How am I gonna do that?

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:04.166
So, then my
perspective just shifted

00:08:04.166 --> 00:08:08.166
on what it means to

00:08:08.166 --> 00:08:10.066
have the luck to be alive

00:08:10.066 --> 00:08:14.767
and to have your
health and to want to

00:08:14.767 --> 00:08:18.433
I don't know, operate with a
different sense of purpose.

00:08:18.433 --> 00:08:20.066
- Nate, right over
there in the pink shirt,

00:08:20.066 --> 00:08:22.567
Nathan, excuse me, he
freaked out when he found out

00:08:22.567 --> 00:08:24.066
that the odds of
being a human are

00:08:24.066 --> 00:08:26.133
four hundred trillion to one.

00:08:26.133 --> 00:08:28.133
That happened to him in
L.A. when we were filming.

00:08:28.133 --> 00:08:30.500
So, he feels that same feeling.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:32.300
(audience laughter)

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:33.300
- Congratulations, Nate.

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:37.400
(audience laughter)

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:41.133
- So, talk about the
early days of the business.

00:08:41.133 --> 00:08:43.667
It's such a big business now,
all of you should

00:08:43.667 --> 00:08:44.734
I don't know if
you take visitors,

00:08:44.734 --> 00:08:45.834
but you should
all visit the offices--

00:08:45.834 --> 00:08:48.934
- Yeah, we do!

00:08:48.934 --> 00:08:51.600
- It's just growing
up so in such a big way.

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:53.300
Obviously, we've
worked with Unilever

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:56.333
and Johnson &amp; Johnson, and it
gets brought up all the time.

00:08:56.333 --> 00:08:57.600
And it's so crazy because--

00:08:57.600 --> 00:08:59.433
- We're like the
little engine that could.

00:08:59.433 --> 00:09:00.500
- That's fine, but you're--

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:02.233
