I'm finding random people,

00:23:24.633 --> 00:23:28.066
and I'm giving them makeovers
on the spot on Facebook.

00:23:28.066 --> 00:23:29.867
- So everybody, next Tuesday
you're allowed to be off,

00:23:29.867 --> 00:23:32.533
pick a random Target,
and hold your breath.

00:23:32.533 --> 00:23:33.967
(audience laughter)

00:23:33.967 --> 00:23:37.967
- And so, today I did
two makeovers on Tuesdays.

00:23:37.967 --> 00:23:38.800
- Did they lose their shit?

00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:40.400
- I was like nervous.

00:23:40.400 --> 00:23:42.700
What if they're
gonna not like it?

00:23:42.700 --> 00:23:44.633
What if they're
gonna say no to me?

00:23:44.633 --> 00:23:46.433
- And sometimes they might
not know who you are, right?

00:23:46.433 --> 00:23:47.400
I mean, there's like
four of those people.

00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:48.900
- Yeah, of course,
yeah, absolutely!

00:23:48.900 --> 00:23:50.934
And they might be like,
"Why are you touching my face?"

00:23:50.934 --> 00:23:52.467
Like, I don't know!

00:23:52.467 --> 00:23:53.300
- And?

00:23:53.300 --> 00:23:54.400
- And they, and it went well.

00:23:54.400 --> 00:23:55.300
- They loved it, yeah.

00:23:55.300 --> 00:23:56.967
(audience laughter)

00:23:56.967 --> 00:23:58.400
- Thank God!

00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:01.700
- Alright, who
else has got a question?

00:24:01.700 --> 00:24:03.333
Dante!

00:24:03.333 --> 00:24:04.700
Get up here, baby!

00:24:04.700 --> 00:24:06.100
That was awesome.

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:09.400
Dante, that was,
yeah, you brought it.

00:24:09.400 --> 00:24:12.467
- [Dante] So, we're going to
be influencer department, here

00:24:12.467 --> 00:24:15.300
and I'm sure that, you know,
we've seen posts from

00:24:15.300 --> 00:24:18.767
big, influential individuals,
people with many followers.

00:24:18.767 --> 00:24:20.867
How do you think that
has contributed to the growth

00:24:20.867 --> 00:24:22.934
of your business on social,

00:24:22.934 --> 00:24:27.100
just having those type of people
speaking about your brand.

00:24:27.100 --> 00:24:28.100
- I think...

00:24:29.500 --> 00:24:31.000
Influencers are tricky.

00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:35.500
I actually feel like
the influencers that have

00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:39.200
the most followers
maybe aren't as trusted

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:42.500
as people who
have a few thousand

00:24:42.500 --> 00:24:45.967
or in the high hundreds.

00:24:45.967 --> 00:24:48.633
Because, to me,
it's like the people who

00:24:48.633 --> 00:24:50.533
have that authentic engagement

00:24:50.533 --> 00:24:55.500
and have that
rabid fan base is more

00:24:55.500 --> 00:24:58.834
the people that are
going to affect change

00:24:58.834 --> 00:25:02.967
versus someone who's
just so giant because--

00:25:02.967 --> 00:25:04.533
- Because they're
pitching products every day,

00:25:04.533 --> 00:25:05.367
is your thought?

00:25:05.367 --> 00:25:08.300
- Or because they're
just part of a machine,

00:25:08.300 --> 00:25:12.033
and most of the people that
are following them are bots.

00:25:12.033 --> 00:25:14.834
We know that that
whole thing can be gamed.

00:25:14.834 --> 00:25:17.133
I was never one of those people.

00:25:17.133 --> 00:25:19.033
I never bought
any of my followers,

00:25:19.033 --> 00:25:21.000
and I do everything myself,

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:24.533
and there's no agency
or other people that

00:25:24.533 --> 00:25:26.633
do, you know--
- Your engagement.

00:25:26.633 --> 00:25:28.133
- My engagement,
but also it was like,

00:25:28.133 --> 00:25:32.567
for me to be honest,
why I'm so involved

00:25:32.567 --> 00:25:35.100
in my online persona is

00:25:35.100 --> 00:25:37.967
because I felt like my entire

00:25:37.967 --> 00:25:40.233
career I was in
someone else's hands.

00:25:40.233 --> 00:25:42.533
So, whether it was Fox's hands--

00:25:42.533 --> 00:25:43.533
- The Hollywood Machine.

00:25:43.533 --> 00:25:48.200
- Or the machine of the
production company Paramount,

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:51.467
or Sony, or whoever, and how
they wanted to market me,

00:25:51.467 --> 00:25:55.667
or the publication that wanted
to do the interview of me.

00:25:55.667 --> 00:25:58.900
I was this version
for the GQ reader,

00:25:58.900 --> 00:26:01.266
and I was that version for
the Seventeen Magazine reader,

00:26:01.266 --> 00:26:03.700
and I was that version for
the People Magazine reader.

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:06.166
No part of it was really me.

00:26:06.166 --> 00:26:07.100
- It was pieces.

00:26:07.100 --> 00:26:09.734
- For the first time,
I really got to tell

00:26:09.734 --> 00:26:11.200
my story, and I got to be real

00:26:11.200 --> 00:26:14.533
and I got to have
that direct relationship

00:26:14.533 --> 00:26:16.900
with myself and have

00:26:16.900 --> 00:26:20.667
control over who I am.

00:26:20.667 --> 00:26:23.500
- The exact quote, the
exact way, to the end user.

00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:25.900
- Yeah, and I never
got to do that before,

00:26:25.900 --> 00:26:28.300
and so, that's why
I care about it so much,

00:26:28.300 --> 00:26:30.800
and that's why
I've been so involved.

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:32.200
- And yeah, Rahm.

00:26:32.200 --> 00:26:34.133
- [Rahm] I think that,

00:26:34.133 --> 00:26:35.900
kind of an interesting segway

00:26:35.900 --> 00:26:37.967
into what I'm curious
about, which is,

00:26:37.967 --> 00:26:40.166
obviously you
have invested so much

00:26:40.166 --> 00:26:42.767
of your personal brand
into The Honest Company.

00:26:42.767 --> 00:26:44.233
You're like, proud of
that and you feel like

00:26:44.233 --> 00:26:47.333
that's kind of your
most authentic self.

00:26:47.333 --> 00:26:50.400
Does it frustrate you if people
would still say, oh yeah,

00:26:50.400 --> 00:26:51.633
like if tonight
someone went home

00:26:51.633 --> 00:26:55.200
and said I saw
Jessica Alba, the actress?

00:26:55.200 --> 00:26:56.867
Or would you feel like

00:26:56.867 --> 00:26:59.433
that's just part
of your broader story?

00:26:59.433 --> 00:27:00.567
- Before you answer that,
I just want to make sure

00:27:00.567 --> 00:27:01.600
the other offices
that that are watching.

00:27:01.600 --> 00:27:04.834
So the question is,
Rahm is asking is,

00:27:04.834 --> 00:27:05.867
if somebody went
home tonight and said,

00:27:05.867 --> 00:27:08.100
"Oh I saw
Jessica Alba, the actress."

00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:10.734
You put so much time and effort
in building this business

00:27:10.734 --> 00:27:12.500
and yourself into it.

00:27:12.500 --> 00:27:14.967
I guess the question is,

00:27:14.967 --> 00:27:19.066
would she be frustrated
being identified as that?

00:27:21.066 --> 00:27:22.033
- I guess it depends on the--

00:27:22.033 --> 00:27:24.367
- In the beginning
probably more, right?

00:27:24.367 --> 00:27:27.400
- No, it really
depends on the context.

00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:29.400
I mean, if people know me more

00:27:29.400 --> 00:27:32.133
from one thing versus another,

00:27:32.133 --> 00:27:36.600
I can't expect them
to not know me for that,

00:27:36.600 --> 00:27:41.300
and I'm grateful that I
was given the opportunity

00:27:41.300 --> 00:27:43.567
to have the
career in entertainment

00:27:43.567 --> 00:27:45.934
that I got to have.

00:27:45.934 --> 00:27:48.066
I actually miss
acting. (laughs)

00:27:48.066 --> 00:27:48.834
It's so weird.

00:27:48.834 --> 00:27:52.166
- We need some people
for some of our videos, so...

00:27:52.166 --> 00:27:53.200
- I'll do it.

00:27:53.200 --> 00:27:54.133
- Great.

00:27:54.133 --> 00:27:54.967
Booked.

00:27:54.967 --> 00:27:56.333
(audience laughter)

00:27:56.333 --> 00:27:58.400
- My husband's
always like, really?

00:27:58.400 --> 00:28:00.200
No I actually do, I miss it.

00:28:00.200 --> 00:28:03.967
- Do you think that
means you'll probably do it?

00:28:03.967 --> 00:28:04.867
- You know, I don't know--

00:28:04.867 --> 00:28:08.600
- Do you think about
71 year old you, acting?

00:28:08.600 --> 00:28:12.633
- Here's the thing when you're
acting, you can always do it

00:28:12.633 --> 00:28:16.567
and you only stop
doing it when you are like,

00:28:16.567 --> 00:28:18.734
alright I never want
to play that role--

00:28:18.734 --> 00:28:20.734
- Right, it's not
like an NBA player right?

00:28:20.734 --> 00:28:22.967
Like you've got that window.
- No you don't have

00:28:22.967 --> 00:28:23.800
an expiration date.

00:28:23.800 --> 00:28:27.800
Now will you always
get paid the same money

00:28:27.800 --> 00:28:30.066
or will you still be the ingeneu
for the rest of your life?

00:28:30.066 --> 00:28:33.233
No, but do you get to
evolve into different,

00:28:33.233 --> 00:28:35.033
more meatier
different types of people?

00:28:35.033 --> 00:28:35.867
Sure.

00:28:35.867 --> 00:28:37.567
- Are you going to
be like a funny grandma?

00:28:37.567 --> 00:28:38.734
- Yeah.

00:28:38.734 --> 00:28:40.233
I'm going to be
the funniest grandma.

00:28:40.233 --> 00:28:42.433
(Gary laughs)
Are you kidding?

00:28:44.133 --> 00:28:46.533
So I miss it, I do.

00:28:46.533 --> 00:28:48.633
Do I care if people
think of me as an actress?

00:28:48.633 --> 00:28:50.867
It depends on the context.

00:28:50.867 --> 00:28:54.967
If someone is just
trying to be mean spirited

00:28:54.967 --> 00:28:59.400
and take away what
I've built, then sure.

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:00.867
It hurts my feelings.

00:29:00.867 --> 00:29:03.700
I am a sensitive person.

00:29:03.700 --> 00:29:05.867
But if someone just
doesn't really know me

00:29:05.867 --> 00:29:09.900
from anything else, then
I'm like alright, that's cool.

00:29:09.900 --> 00:29:11.300
- Good.

00:29:11.300 --> 00:29:12.800
Anybody else?

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:14.066
Yep, great, hey Elise.

00:29:14.066 --> 00:29:17.433
- [Elise] Hi. What brings
you the most joy?

00:29:17.433 --> 00:29:19.433
Would you say it's your acting

00:29:19.433 --> 00:29:22.500
or would you say it's
your business venture,

00:29:22.500 --> 00:29:23.700
or something else?

00:29:25.400 --> 00:29:29.100
- I probably have like
an unhealthy relationship

00:29:29.100 --> 00:29:30.967
with acting.

00:29:30.967 --> 00:29:33.734
It's like the boyfriend who's
never really that into you.

00:29:33.734 --> 00:29:34.700
(audience laughter)

00:29:34.700 --> 00:29:37.266
- Tell me more.
(audience laughter)

00:29:37.266 --> 00:29:38.700
- Exactly like Gary.

00:29:38.700 --> 00:29:41.734
(audience laughter)

00:29:41.734 --> 00:29:44.734
Just want him to not look
over my shoulder in (mumbles)

00:29:44.734 --> 00:29:48.066
this time, but he's always
interested in what's going on.

00:29:48.066 --> 00:29:51.867
- It's true.
Where's the camera?

00:29:51.867 --> 00:29:56.166
- Yeah, I have to say
though, as far as legacy,

00:29:56.166 --> 00:29:59.300
in all seriousness,
and personal fulfillment,

00:29:59.300 --> 00:30:01.967
I've never, I've never even,

00:30:03.834 --> 00:30:06.700
I've never thought
that I would be able to

00:30:06.700 --> 00:30:10.166
have such a full life.

00:30:10.166 --> 00:30:11.767
And being an entrepreneur

00:30:11.767 --> 00:30:15.567
and being able to
create a company

00:30:15.567 --> 00:30:16.834
and create a vision

00:30:16.834 --> 00:30:18.300
and create solutions for people

00:30:18.300 --> 00:30:20.867
that'll really
transform their lives.

00:30:20.867 --> 00:30:25.066
And also to watch people

00:30:25.066 --> 00:30:27.200
really grow up
in their own lives,

00:30:27.200 --> 00:30:28.800
in their own careers.

00:30:28.800 --> 00:30:32.834
And watch personal development
on such an intimate level.

00:30:32.834 --> 00:30:36.567
'Cause prior to this,
I would spend a lot of time,

00:30:36.567 --> 00:30:39.533
with strangers,
for about four months,

00:30:39.533 --> 00:30:41.500
and then I'd
never see them again.

00:30:41.500 --> 00:30:44.166
But the time that I spent
with them was every day,

00:30:44.166 --> 00:30:46.300
all day and night.

00:30:46.300 --> 00:30:48.433
- And it was the best.
- And it was the best.

00:30:48.433 --> 00:30:50.166
And you create those,

00:30:50.166 --> 00:30:53.967
some really intimate
relationships quickly,

00:30:53.967 --> 00:30:57.834
but then they go away
and it was so fleeting.

00:30:57.834 --> 00:30:59.166
But there's
something so permanent

00:30:59.166 --> 00:31:00.967
about going into
the office everyday

00:31:00.967 --> 00:31:03.467
and I see the same
people that I saw five years ago

00:31:03.467 --> 00:31:05.367
and I see who they are today

00:31:05.367 --> 00:31:08.500
versus who they were when
they walked in five years ago

00:31:08.500 --> 00:31:12.667
and that's so dope,
just to see how people evolve

00:31:12.667 --> 00:31:15.200
and to have those
personal connections.

00:31:15.200 --> 00:31:17.166
- Love that.

00:31:17.166 --> 00:31:18.900
Yep?

00:31:18.900 --> 00:31:20.600
Rebec, you can
come up to the mic.

00:31:20.600 --> 00:31:23.000
Oh, you've got one? Go ahead.

00:31:24.800 --> 00:31:27.367
Go ahead, go ahead,
go ahead, go ahead.

00:31:27.367 --> 00:31:29.300
- [Man] You've done a
lot of really cool work,

00:31:29.300 --> 00:31:31.533
specifically with kids,

00:31:31.533 --> 00:31:32.567
what's the most powerful

00:31:32.567 --> 00:31:35.100
experience that
you've had helping kids,

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:37.200
and how has that
impacted the other choices

00:31:37.200 --> 00:31:39.467
that you make in your life?

00:31:42.767 --> 00:31:43.600
- Wow.

00:31:45.400 --> 00:31:46.667
- [Man] Curveball.

00:31:46.667 --> 00:31:48.834
- Yeah, I know, this
is a loaded question,

00:31:48.834 --> 00:31:52.467
because I feel like

00:31:52.467 --> 00:31:55.533
I'm 100 percent

00:31:55.533 --> 00:31:56.533
have dedicated my life

00:31:56.533 --> 00:31:58.934
in a weird way to helping kids,

00:31:58.934 --> 00:32:00.233
and this is the
first time that I actually

00:32:00.233 --> 00:32:02.300
kind of thought
about it that way, but--

00:32:02.300 --> 00:32:03.500
- Dude.

00:32:03.500 --> 00:32:05.800
(audience laughter)

00:32:05.800 --> 00:32:06.633
- This is huge.

00:32:06.633 --> 00:32:10.300
(audience laughing and applause)

00:32:10.300 --> 00:32:13.767
- I was a really sick
kid when I was younger,

00:32:13.767 --> 00:32:16.100
and I wanted to,

00:32:16.100 --> 00:32:18.166
literally anything
would be better

00:32:18.166 --> 00:32:20.900
than to be sick and
to be in the hospital

00:32:20.900 --> 00:32:24.333
and to have surgery, and
I had multiple surgeries,

00:32:24.333 --> 00:32:26.266
and multiple health problems.

00:32:26.266 --> 00:32:29.200
And I just, I literally just
wanted to be anyone else,

00:32:29.200 --> 00:32:31.367
which was why
I wanted to be an actor.

00:32:31.367 --> 00:32:34.533
Because it was like a way
for that to actually be real,

00:32:34.533 --> 00:32:37.967
as real as it can be,
and certainly be a superhero,

00:32:37.967 --> 00:32:40.000
and be larger than life.

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:43.700
'Cause I was like, they
can overcome anything!

00:32:43.700 --> 00:32:48.066
And so, yeah, from helping,

00:32:48.066 --> 00:32:52.400
being able to
provide families with

00:32:52.400 --> 00:32:55.033
products is one thing,

00:32:55.033 --> 00:32:57.533
but even just basic essentials.

00:32:58.600 --> 00:33:01.900
Infant mortality is a
huge issue in this country,

00:33:01.900 --> 00:33:04.000
and a lot of babies get

00:33:04.000 --> 00:33:05.633
suffocated while
they're sleeping,

00:33:05.633 --> 00:33:08.700
because parents
can't provide a safe place

00:33:08.700 --> 00:33:10.967
for them to sleep,
and we did an initiative

00:33:10.967 --> 00:33:14.266
where we provided, and we
still have an ongoing thing

00:33:14.266 --> 00:33:17.367
among the board of this
organization called Baby2Baby,

00:33:17.367 --> 00:33:21.367
and one of our big
initiatives is to give families

00:33:21.367 --> 00:33:23.900
a safe place for
their new baby to sleep,

00:33:23.900 --> 00:33:26.200
so that they never
have to worry about that.

00:33:26.200 --> 00:33:28.033
That's a real thing, 

00:33:28.033 --> 00:33:32.133
and that's
incredibly meaningful.

00:33:32.133 --> 00:33:36.266
I also got the opportunity
to go to Africa with Bono,

00:33:36.266 --> 00:33:38.700
and Queen Rania
around the World Cup,

00:33:38.700 --> 00:33:43.433
to fight for education reform,

00:33:43.433 --> 00:33:45.033
a global education fund

00:33:45.033 --> 00:33:48.133
so that every
person had access

00:33:48.133 --> 00:33:50.900
to a comprehensive
education, and most people who

00:33:50.900 --> 00:33:53.567
don't have access are women.

00:33:53.567 --> 00:33:55.333
So that is
incredibly meaningful,

00:33:55.333 --> 00:33:56.734
and being able to give

00:33:56.734 --> 00:34:01.033
girls an opportunity to
just empower themselves to

00:34:01.033 --> 00:34:03.233
get themselves
out of the situation

00:34:03.233 --> 00:34:06.500
that they don't
want to be in, and change

00:34:06.500 --> 00:34:09.133
their family's
life and their own life.

00:34:09.133 --> 00:34:12.433
There's been a lot
of these circumstances.

00:34:14.333 --> 00:34:18.100
Yeah, just thinking
about it, it's very cool

00:34:18.100 --> 00:34:20.900
because I don't
often get to, but probably

00:34:20.900 --> 00:34:24.300
raising my own little
stinkers has probably been

00:34:24.300 --> 00:34:28.233
the most meaningful,
but being able to

00:34:28.233 --> 00:34:31.700
be part of so much change,

00:34:31.700 --> 00:34:33.800
and as scary and weird

00:34:33.800 --> 00:34:37.166
and crazy as our
country feels right now,

00:34:37.166 --> 00:34:41.967
with certain political things,

00:34:41.967 --> 00:34:45.900
I feel like there's
enough of us out there

00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:50.367
that walk with integrity

00:34:50.367 --> 00:34:53.233
and care about the right things,

00:34:53.233 --> 00:34:55.867
and we're going to
continue to be progressive

00:34:55.867 --> 00:34:59.300
in how we operate,
and that we'll always win.

00:34:59.300 --> 00:35:00.500
- Awesome.

00:35:00.500 --> 00:35:02.767
(audience applause)

00:35:02.767 --> 00:35:05.467
- I think the thing
that I have learned

00:35:05.467 --> 00:35:09.433
that no one told me, it's
sort of like being a parent.

00:35:09.433 --> 00:35:14.300
No one tells you
what you're really in for

00:35:14.300 --> 00:35:16.867
as far as the sleepless nights,

00:35:16.867 --> 00:35:21.433
and you'll never not
worry again thing that happens.

00:35:21.433 --> 00:35:26.166
In business, you never
underestimate your gut.

00:35:27.233 --> 00:35:30.700
There's a lot that
you can learn in school

00:35:30.700 --> 00:35:33.433
and through experience,
and making mistakes,

00:35:33.433 --> 00:35:38.166
but trusting your gut and
knowing what you don't know

00:35:38.166 --> 00:35:41.400
is just as important as
knowing what you do know.

00:35:41.400 --> 00:35:44.433
And being able to have
the humility to support

00:35:44.433 --> 00:35:48.467
what you don't know
with experts and other people

00:35:48.467 --> 00:35:52.967
to fill those gaps,
and trusting your gut.

00:35:52.967 --> 00:35:54.934
- Love it.

00:35:54.934 --> 00:35:56.200
Go ahead.

00:35:56.200 --> 00:35:57.734
Here, grab this, yeah.

00:35:57.734 --> 00:35:58.567
- [Tatiana] I don't need a mic.

00:35:58.567 --> 00:36:01.000
- Go ahead, but they need it.

00:36:03.100 --> 00:36:04.467
- [Tatiana] Thank you.

00:36:04.467 --> 00:36:06.033
Hi, my name is Tatiana.

00:36:06.033 --> 00:36:09.066
I'm Peruvian-American,
I'm Latina, so

00:36:09.066 --> 00:36:11.200
my question is going
to be related to that.

00:36:11.200 --> 00:36:15.000
I think today, it's
exciting to see representation

00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:17.567
of our mission both
onscreen and off where

00:36:17.567 --> 00:36:18.867
there wasn't always.

00:36:18.867 --> 00:36:20.033
- Yeah.

00:36:20.033 --> 00:36:21.166
- [Tatiana] That being said,

00:36:21.166 --> 00:36:24.033
it hasn't always
been easy and I think,

00:36:24.033 --> 00:36:26.367
especially with
today's political climate,

00:36:26.367 --> 00:36:28.400
what I would love to know is

00:36:28.400 --> 00:36:31.667
what advice do you have for
young Latina entrepreneurs

00:36:31.667 --> 00:36:34.633
that are looking to build
businesses and be successful?

00:36:34.633 --> 00:36:36.867
If you faced any
specific challenges

00:36:36.867 --> 00:36:39.367
or advantages in that respect?

00:36:43.500 --> 00:36:45.433
- Advantages, no.

00:36:45.433 --> 00:36:46.800
(audience laughter)

00:36:46.800 --> 00:36:49.467
You'd have to be a

00:36:49.467 --> 00:36:52.367
 white guy if
you want advantages.

00:36:52.367 --> 00:36:53.700
(audience chuckles)

00:36:53.700 --> 00:36:57.266
I mean, that's
just the way it is.

00:36:57.266 --> 00:37:01.066
It helps if you're
tall and good-looking, too.

00:37:03.000 --> 00:37:08.233
You know, being a girl who's
just trying to figure it out

00:37:08.233 --> 00:37:09.567
and doesn't, you know, for me,

00:37:09.567 --> 00:37:11.200
I'm just talking
from my experience,

00:37:11.200 --> 00:37:15.033
I never had a traditiona

00:37:15.033 --> 00:37:16.233
lor conventional background

00:37:16.233 --> 00:37:19.700
or education, nor did 
I have nepotism that got me

00:37:19.700 --> 00:37:20.734
to where I'm at,

00:37:20.734 --> 00:37:24.367
and I literally
had to pay my own way,

00:37:24.367 --> 00:37:28.266
and I had to figure it out

00:37:28.266 --> 00:37:31.166
and create that path and
have the vision for myself.

00:37:31.166 --> 00:37:33.834
It was almost the
naysayers that drove me

00:37:33.834 --> 00:37:36.066
to prove them wrong.

00:37:36.066 --> 00:37:40.633
And also, when you don't
have anywhere to go but up,

00:37:40.633 --> 00:37:43.266
there's almost

00:37:43.266 --> 00:37:44.934
something nice about that

00:37:44.934 --> 00:37:47.100
because people
don't expect you to fail

00:37:47.100 --> 00:37:48.266
because they don't
think you're capable

00:37:48.266 --> 00:37:50.767
of doing anything
more than nothing.

00:37:50.767 --> 00:37:54.767
And so, then,
there's even more of a fire

00:37:54.767 --> 00:37:57.667
to prove to them that you can.

00:37:57.667 --> 00:37:59.967
And that you can
make a difference.

00:37:59.967 --> 00:38:01.667
What can you do?

00:38:01.667 --> 00:38:04.433
I think you have to

00:38:04.433 --> 00:38:06.100
first, really
figure out what it is

00:38:06.100 --> 00:38:08.100
you're trying
to do in the world.

00:38:08.100 --> 00:38:11.066
How are you
going to be different?

00:38:11.066 --> 00:38:12.500
Look at a competitive landscape

00:38:12.500 --> 00:38:15.233
be very realistic with yourself.

00:38:15.233 --> 00:38:17.700
And make sure that you

00:38:17.700 --> 00:38:21.333
have a very unique proposition

00:38:21.333 --> 00:38:23.900
or perspective or point of view

00:38:23.900 --> 00:38:27.667
because if you're
just a "me, too" company...

00:38:27.667 --> 00:38:28.600
Why?

00:38:28.600 --> 00:38:29.900
Why do it?

00:38:29.900 --> 00:38:31.166
- I think the one
thing that I would add just

00:38:31.166 --> 00:38:33.133
I'll jump into this one
'cause I love it so much.

00:38:33.133 --> 00:38:35.100
I think the one thing
that true entrepreneurs need

00:38:35.100 --> 00:38:39.400
to realize is that
the market doesn't care.

00:38:39.400 --> 00:38:42.967
Let me tell you one
great thing about the market,

00:38:42.967 --> 00:38:45.533
it wants to make money.

00:38:45.533 --> 00:38:47.633
Do I think some VCs may say,

00:38:47.633 --> 00:38:48.867
"You don't look the part, no"?

00:38:48.867 --> 00:38:50.967
Sure, but I've got a news alert.

00:38:50.967 --> 00:38:54.300
I don't usually invest
in white, affluent dudes

00:38:54.300 --> 00:38:55.967
from good families because
I don't think they have a chip

00:38:55.967 --> 00:38:57.033
on their shoulder.

00:38:57.033 --> 00:38:58.500
So, I'm playing the reverse.

00:38:58.500 --> 00:39:01.133
And the truth is, I've been
wrong, and I've been right.

00:39:01.133 --> 00:39:02.467
So, I think the one great

00:39:02.467 --> 00:39:04.266
if you're a
pure-bred entrepreneur,

00:39:04.266 --> 00:39:06.433
you realize you're getting
punched in the fucking mouth

00:39:06.433 --> 00:39:08.467
24/7, 365 anyway.

00:39:08.467 --> 00:39:09.834
The market is the market,

00:39:09.834 --> 00:39:12.600
and if you dwell
on what you are,

00:39:12.600 --> 00:39:15.066
and why you're not
winning because of that,

00:39:15.066 --> 00:39:15.900
you would have never had a,

00:39:15.900 --> 00:39:17.233
it's no different than
the people we said no to.

00:39:17.233 --> 00:39:18.834
- You can't, sorry--
- Please.

00:39:18.834 --> 00:39:21.367
- You can't be a
victim of circumstance.

00:39:21.367 --> 00:39:23.967
It's sort of like
blaming your parents

00:39:23.967 --> 00:39:25.867
it's like yeah, right.

00:39:25.867 --> 00:39:28.433
Everyone has a history
and you can blame your parents

00:39:28.433 --> 00:39:29.767
until you're blue in the face,

00:39:29.767 --> 00:39:32.500
but if you only
work on, focus on that,

00:39:32.500 --> 00:39:35.233
you'll never get
over it and be an adult,

00:39:35.233 --> 00:39:38.000
and figure your own life out.

00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:39.166
And, by the way,
your parents are just

00:39:39.166 --> 00:39:41.800
trying the best they could,
and there's no manual,

00:39:41.800 --> 00:39:43.767
which I learned later in life.

00:39:43.767 --> 00:39:46.600
(audience laughter)

00:39:47.967 --> 00:39:50.533
I think if I would've
started The Honest Company

00:39:50.533 --> 00:39:54.033
when I wanted to start,
it would've been too early.

00:39:54.033 --> 00:39:58.467
And actually having
three years of rejection

00:39:58.467 --> 00:40:00.767
it gave me the

00:40:00.767 --> 00:40:04.033
armor to take on

00:40:04.033 --> 00:40:06.000
actually getting funding

00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:07.200
and starting the company,

00:40:07.200 --> 00:40:10.333
and taking all of
the punches in my face

00:40:10.333 --> 00:40:12.633
and my eyes and
my ear and my nose

00:40:12.633 --> 00:40:14.367
that constantly came at me.

00:40:14.367 --> 00:40:15.800
- And still do, everyday.

00:40:15.800 --> 00:40:18.800
- And still do,
everyday, of course.

00:40:18.800 --> 00:40:21.967
But I have more calluses

00:40:21.967 --> 00:40:25.500
and so I can take it
differently than I could have.

00:40:25.500 --> 00:40:29.200
- We'll sneak in 
two quick ones, sorry Jess. Pam.

00:40:29.200 --> 00:40:32.667
- [Pam] Hey, I'm Pamela, and
thank you again for being here.

00:40:32.667 --> 00:40:35.033
My question is a little
bit more about the blend

00:40:35.033 --> 00:40:37.367
of your personal life
and your professional life

00:40:37.367 --> 00:40:41.066
and what a typical
day looks like for you

00:40:41.066 --> 00:40:43.300
if there is a typical day.

00:40:43.300 --> 00:40:46.900
And then, if you have any
rules of thumb or tips

00:40:46.900 --> 00:40:49.166
and ways that you keep
your friendships alive,

00:40:49.166 --> 00:40:52.767
your relationship and
your family time protected,

00:40:52.767 --> 00:40:56.500
and also the type of
passion projects in your job.

00:40:56.500 --> 00:40:58.500
- Does it look like I
have it all figured out?

00:40:58.500 --> 00:40:59.667
(audience laughter)

00:40:59.667 --> 00:41:02.300
'Cause I really don't.

00:41:02.300 --> 00:41:05.667
I'm always everyday just

00:41:05.667 --> 00:41:07.867
trying to do the best I can.

00:41:07.867 --> 00:41:10.266
No two days are the same.

00:41:10.266 --> 00:41:12.867
I'm constantly
trying to create routines

00:41:12.867 --> 00:41:14.700
'cause I feel likes
that's good, and everything

00:41:14.700 --> 00:41:17.300
that I've read
about successful people is

00:41:17.300 --> 00:41:19.400
that they start early.

00:41:19.400 --> 00:41:21.133
I hate waking up early.

00:41:21.133 --> 00:41:23.867
It hurts all of my feelings.

00:41:23.867 --> 00:41:26.633
Every time I wake up early

00:41:26.633 --> 00:41:28.600
it's just like why me.

00:41:28.600 --> 00:41:29.800
(audience laughter)

00:41:29.800 --> 00:41:32.333
But I've read so much
that successful people.

00:41:32.333 --> 00:41:35.200
I feel like you start early.

00:41:35.200 --> 00:41:38.166
- It's not early or late.

00:41:38.166 --> 00:41:41.533
For me it's just
maximizing when I'm awake.

00:41:41.533 --> 00:41:43.367
People are always
like how much do you sleep?

00:41:43.367 --> 00:41:44.800
Thinking I'm
gonna say three or four.

00:41:44.800 --> 00:41:46.834
I'm dying to get six or seven

00:41:46.834 --> 00:41:48.033
'cause I think it's healthy.

00:41:48.033 --> 00:41:48.834
- Yeah you need it. you need it.

00:41:48.834 --> 00:41:51.834
- It's just that I'm
fucking on all 18 hours

00:41:51.834 --> 00:41:53.100
when I'm awake, right?

00:41:53.100 --> 00:41:55.367
- Yeah, he is.

00:41:55.367 --> 00:41:56.433
(audience laughter)

00:41:56.433 --> 00:41:57.467
- Oh, they know.

00:41:57.467 --> 00:42:00.467
(Jessica laughs)

00:42:00.467 --> 00:42:04.633
- I wish I figured
out the secret sauce.

00:42:04.633 --> 00:42:06.233
I don't know.

00:42:06.233 --> 00:42:08.433
I have friends that

00:42:08.433 --> 00:42:11.066
I was really close with

00:42:11.066 --> 00:42:14.000
at certain periods of time,
and now we're not,

00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:16.667
and now I have other friends
that I'm really close with.

00:42:16.667 --> 00:42:19.166
I kind of give myself a break.

00:42:20.233 --> 00:42:22.900
(audience laughter)

00:42:22.900 --> 00:42:24.934
I just think that if you can

00:42:24.934 --> 00:42:27.533
not think of the pressure

00:42:27.533 --> 00:42:29.500
of society and
who you need to be,

00:42:29.500 --> 00:42:32.667
and you're just really true
with who you are and know

00:42:32.667 --> 00:42:35.000
that you're trying
to do the best you can,

00:42:35.000 --> 00:42:36.567
you can kind of
just figure it out.

00:42:36.567 --> 00:42:38.500
That's my theory.

00:42:38.500 --> 00:42:42.400
It's probably wrong,
but I wish I was better

00:42:42.400 --> 00:42:45.967
at waking up early, and
eating healthy, and exercising,

00:42:45.967 --> 00:42:47.066
and calling my mom.

00:42:47.066 --> 00:42:49.834
I'm just not good at stuff,

00:42:49.834 --> 00:42:53.133
but I'm good at
being with my kids

00:42:53.133 --> 00:42:57.166
and telling them about things,
and when they yell at me

00:42:57.166 --> 00:42:59.834
enough about the same
thing over and over again,

00:42:59.834 --> 00:43:02.500
I can sometimes
say okay, you're right.

00:43:02.500 --> 00:43:04.433
I'm probably
doing the wrong thing,

00:43:04.433 --> 00:43:08.166
and I'll try to
do better next time.

00:43:08.166 --> 00:43:09.767
- [Jermaine] Hi,
my name is Jermaine.

00:43:09.767 --> 00:43:11.600
My question is more
on entrepreneurship.

00:43:11.600 --> 00:43:13.500
What are your top three
factors that inspired you

00:43:13.500 --> 00:43:15.367
to become an entrepreneur?

00:43:15.367 --> 00:43:19.400
And what order would you
rank those three factors?

00:43:19.400 --> 00:43:24.066
- Three factors that inspired
me to be an entrepreneur.

00:43:24.066 --> 00:43:27.533
Social justice was my big one.

00:43:27.533 --> 00:43:31.600
I felt like it was completely
unfair that only people

00:43:31.600 --> 00:43:35.000
who lived in a
certain tax bracket

00:43:35.000 --> 00:43:39.867
deserved to have a
better life and had access

00:43:39.867 --> 00:43:43.500
to not just better products,
but more education

00:43:43.500 --> 00:43:47.533
to make better choices.

00:43:47.533 --> 00:43:50.934
I would say that's
one, two, and three.

00:43:50.934 --> 00:43:54.867
I went and lobbied
for chemical reform

00:43:54.867 --> 00:43:57.667
and tried to work at that angle,

00:43:57.667 --> 00:43:59.400
and I got nowhere.

00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:01.233
They made it a partisan issue,

00:44:01.233 --> 00:44:03.233
and it's a human health issue.

00:44:03.233 --> 00:44:06.567
When you're like
this is a civil right.

00:44:06.567 --> 00:44:08.000
People should just
know what's in stuff

00:44:08.000 --> 00:44:09.567
and how it's going
to effect their health,

00:44:09.567 --> 00:44:13.200
and the fact that the
government won't protect us,

00:44:13.200 --> 00:44:14.967
someone needs to
go out there and prove

00:44:14.967 --> 00:44:18.233
that people want this,
and people will vote

00:44:18.233 --> 00:44:20.533
with their wallets.

00:44:20.533 --> 00:44:23.734
That's what really
drove me everyday,

00:44:23.734 --> 00:44:26.033
and that's what still drives me.

00:44:26.033 --> 00:44:27.800
- Jess, thank you so
much for stopping by.

00:44:27.800 --> 00:44:29.166
- Yeah, thank you.
- I love you.

00:44:29.166 --> 00:44:31.400
(audience applause)

